QUESTION TO ANSWER:
How can quantum rotation-based mechanisms be used to build secure digital image encryption, and how do randomness generation, key sensitivity, and pixel transformation processes help resist statistical and differential attacks?

PAPER TITLES AND CLAIMS:
{
  "papers": [
    {
      "title": "A novel image encryption scheme based on quantum dynamical spinning and rotations",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "A novel image encryption scheme is developed based on quantum dynamical spinning and rotation operators, which utilizes quantum mechanics principles such as superposition and entanglement to enhance security and overcome the limitations of classical cryptographic algorithms."
        },
        {
          "text": "The proposed encryption algorithm incorporates both confusion and diffusion processes by employing quantum rotation operators and half-spin representations to encrypt digital images, ensuring high security and resistance to cryptanalysis."
        },
        {
          "text": "The encryption scheme uses a symmetric cryptographic approach where the encryption and decryption processes rely on secret keys and phase data, making it resistant to brute force and differential attacks."
        },
        {
          "text": "The proposed encryption method demonstrates robustness against statistical and differential attacks, as evidenced by uniform histograms, high entropy values close to the theoretical maximum of 8, and high NPCR and UACI values."
        },
        {
          "text": "The encryption scheme allows for flexible key expansion or compression by multiplying with nonsingular matrices, creating a high level of complexity and making it nearly impossible for cryptanalysts to crack the encryption without knowledge of the specific matrices and phase data used."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "RETRACTED: A novel image encryption scheme based on quantum dynamical spinning and rotations",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "A novel image encryption scheme based on quantum rotation and spinning operators introduces confusion and diffusion capabilities, enabling secure encryption of digital images by leveraging quantum mechanics principles such as phase data and rotational matrices."
        },
        {
          "text": "The proposed encryption algorithm achieves high security by utilizing quantum half-spin operators and phase data to encrypt keys and digital images, making it resistant to cryptanalysis and differential attacks."
        },
        {
          "text": "The encryption scheme demonstrates robustness against statistical and differential attacks by producing encrypted images with uniform pixel distributions and negligible correlation coefficients between original and encrypted images."
        },
        {
          "text": "The proposed encryption method achieves high randomness and entropy in encrypted images, passing NIST randomness tests and achieving entropy values close to the theoretical maximum of 8."
        },
        {
          "text": "The encryption algorithm provides flexibility and enhanced security by allowing dynamic key expansion or compression through nonsingular matrices and infinite phase combinations within the range of −720° to 720°."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Single Qubit Quantum Logistic-Sine XYZ-Rotation Maps: An Ultra-Wide Range Dynamics for Image Encryption",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "The Single Qubit Quantum Logistic-Sine XYZ-Rotation Maps (SQQLSR) integrates quantum superposition and quantum rotations along X, Y, and Z axes to generate one-dimensional chaotic sequences with ultra-wide parameter ranges, achieving enhanced randomness, sensitivity to initial conditions, and a significantly expanded key space of up to 10^40."
        },
        {
          "text": "The SQQLSR achieves a maximum Lyapunov exponent of approximately 55.265, surpassing traditional chaotic maps in unpredictability and ensuring a uniform chaotic distribution without periodic windows."
        },
        {
          "text": "The SQQLSR-based image encryption method, which combines permutation and substitution techniques, demonstrates strong randomness with an average entropy of 7.9994, NPCR above 99.6%, and UACI within the range of 32.8%–33.8%, confirming its robustness against statistical and differential attacks."
        },
        {
          "text": "The SQQLSR encryption system exhibits high robustness against noise, cropping, and JPEG compression, ensuring reliable decryption and perfect image reconstruction with an infinite PSNR value."
        },
        {
          "text": "The SQQLSR method, designed for lightweight quantum cryptography, offers a computationally efficient single-qubit framework while maintaining strong chaotic properties, making it suitable for secure communications and resource-constrained environments."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Write a concise but sufficiently detailed answer grounded only in the claims above.

