      "title": "LINC00346 Sponges miR-30c-2-3p to Promote the Development of Lung Adenocarcinoma by Targeting MYBL2 and Regulating CELL CYCLE Signaling Pathway",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "LINC00346 is upregulated in lung adenocarcinoma tissues and cells, where it acts as a competing endogenous RNA to promote tumor progression by sponging miR-30c-2-3p and targeting MYBL2, thereby regulating the cell cycle signaling pathway."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "MiR-204-3p overexpression inhibits gastric carcinoma cell proliferation by inhibiting the MAPK pathway and RIP1/MLK1 necroptosis pathway to promote apoptosis",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "Gastric adenocarcinoma has a poor prognosis due to late-stage diagnosis, and its risk factors include Helicobacter pylori infection, family history of gastric cancer, and pernicious anemia, with progression involving chronic gastritis, gastric atrophy, gastric intestinal metaplasia, and dysplasia."
        },
        {
          "text": "High-quality endoscopy with image-enhanced techniques and systematic biopsy sampling following the Sydney protocol is the most effective method for detecting and risk-stratifying gastric atrophy and gastric intestinal metaplasia."
        },
        {
          "text": "Endoscopic surveillance every three years is recommended for patients with extensive gastric atrophy or gastric intestinal metaplasia affecting both the antrum and body, while surveillance is not recommended for those with changes limited to the antrum unless additional risk factors are present."
        },
        {
          "text": "Endoscopic mucosal resection or endoscopic submucosal dissection is effective for treating visible gastric dysplasia and early gastric cancer, provided specific quality criteria are met, including en bloc resection for lesions larger than 10 mm."
        },
        {
          "text": "There is insufficient evidence to support the use of biomarkers for population screening of gastric adenocarcinoma in low-incidence regions, such as the UK, but biomarkers may help identify patients with chronic atrophic gastritis."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "MiR-378a-3p acts as a tumor suppressor in gastric cancer via directly targeting RAB31 and inhibiting the Hedgehog pathway proteins GLI1/2",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "MiR-378a-3p acts as a tumor suppressor in gastric cancer by directly targeting RAB31, inhibiting the Hedgehog signaling pathway proteins GLI1/2, and reducing the stemness of gastric cancer stem cells."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "MicroRNA-219-2-3p Functions as a Tumor Suppressor in Gastric Cancer and Is Regulated by DNA Methylation",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "MicroRNA-219-2-3p functions as a tumor suppressor in gastric cancer by inhibiting cell proliferation, migration, and invasion, and inducing apoptosis."
        },
        {
          "text": "The expression of microRNA-219-2-3p is downregulated in gastric cancer tissues and cell lines, and its lower expression is associated with higher tumor grade and later clinical stages."
        },
        {
          "text": "The downregulation of microRNA-219-2-3p in gastric cancer is partially regulated by DNA methylation and histone deacetylation of its upstream CpG island."
        },
        {
          "text": "Overexpression of microRNA-219-2-3p in gastric cancer cells reduces the activation of the ERK1/2 signaling pathway, which is implicated in tumor progression."
        },
        {
          "text": "MicroRNA-219-2-3p may regulate gastric cancer progression through targeting oncogenes and apoptosis-related genes, including ERBB3 and MAPK8."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Decrease of miR-202-3p Expression, a Novel Tumor Suppressor, in Gastric Cancer",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "MiR-202-3p is frequently downregulated in gastric cancer tissues compared to adjacent non-tumor tissues, and its expression levels are negatively correlated with tumor size and positively correlated with patient age."
        },
        {
          "text": "Overexpression of miR-202-3p in gastric cancer cells suppresses cell proliferation and induces apoptosis both in vitro and in vivo."
        },
        {
          "text": "The transcription factor Gli1 is a direct target of miR-202-3p, and miR-202-3p downregulates Gli1 expression at the translational level, leading to reduced expression of Gli1 target genes such as c-catenin and BCL-2."
        },
        {
          "text": "Overexpression of Gli1 partially rescues the growth inhibition and apoptosis induced by miR-202-3p in gastric cancer cells, indicating that Gli1 mediates the biological effects of miR-202-3p."
        },
        {
          "text": "MiR-202-3p functions as a tumor suppressor in gastric cancer by directly targeting and inhibiting Gli1, suggesting its potential as a therapeutic target for gastric cancer treatment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A Review on the Role of SPRY4-IT1 in the Carcinogenesis",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "The long non-coding RNA SPRY4-IT1, encoded by a gene on 5q31.3, plays a role in regulating cell growth, proliferation, apoptosis, and lipid biosynthesis, and is implicated in the carcinogenesis of various cancers through mechanisms such as miRNA sponging and interactions with signaling pathways like HIF-1a, NF-kB/p65, AMPK, ZEB1, MAPK, and PI3K/Akt."
        },
        {
          "text": "SPRY4-IT1 exhibits oncogenic roles in multiple cancers, including colorectal, breast, gastric, osteosarcoma, lung, cervical, melanoma, glioma, pancreatic, bladder, liver, and esophageal cancers, by promoting proliferation, migration, invasion, epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and stemness, while its silencing often results in reduced tumor growth and increased apoptosis."
        },
        {
          "text": "SPRY4-IT1 acts as a competing endogenous RNA by sequestering miRNAs such as miR-101-3p, miR-6882-3p, and miR-22-3p, thereby modulating the expression of target genes and contributing to tumor progression in various cancer types."
        },
        {
          "text": "SPRY4-IT1 expression levels are associated with cancer prognosis, with upregulation generally linked to poor clinical outcomes, larger tumor size, advanced stages, and metastasis in most cancers, although contradictory roles have been observed in lung, ovarian, and gastric cancers."
        },
        {
          "text": "SPRY4-IT1 has potential as a diagnostic and prognostic biomarker for various cancers, including melanoma, cervical cancer, and esophageal squamous cell carcinoma, and may serve as a target for therapeutic interventions, though its tissue-specific effects require further investigation."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

