          "text": "Traumatic spinal cord injury induces a robust, long-lasting, and spatially extensive proliferation of central canal ependymal cells, with the proliferative wave peaking at different times depending on the distance from the lesion site."
        },
        {
          "text": "Neither chemically-induced focal demyelination nor autoimmune-mediated multifocal demyelination triggers proliferation of central canal ependymal cells, despite the occurrence of demyelination, remyelination, and significant locomotor deficits in these models."
        },
        {
          "text": "Ependymal cell proliferation following spinal cord injury is asymmetric, with significant increases observed rostral to the lesion site but not caudal to it."
        },
        {
          "text": "Ependymal cells in the adult mammalian spinal cord exhibit latent neural stem cell properties, including the ability to proliferate and differentiate into astrocytes and oligodendrocytes following spinal cord injury."
        },
        {
          "text": "The absence of ependymal cell proliferation in non-traumatic demyelinating conditions suggests that demyelination, remyelination, or autoimmunity alone are insufficient to activate these cells."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

